**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0001"],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.9"]}
- Reused only for authority/context: the Leader’s appointment powers matter for social-service stewardship, but this is not direct welfare performance evidence.

**New evidence**
- UNICEF’s 2017-2022 Iran country programme frames the joint UNICEF-Iran work as children’s wellbeing, health, and education support under national priorities, with the document published in May 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF Country Programme of Cooperation in the Islamic Republic of Iran, 2017 - 2022","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"2022-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/reports/unicef-country-programme-cooperation-islamic-republic-iran-2017-2022-0","claim":"The country programme says UNICEF and Iran cooperated from 2017 to 2022 to promote children's wellbeing, health, and education in line with national priorities.","locator":"page highlights and file listing","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UNICEF country programme page with a clear target-period match, but it is a program descriptor rather than a ruler-specific policy decree.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly covers the target period window through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it reflects state cooperation under the regime, not a personal Khamenei instruction.","contrary_evidence":["Program framing does not prove implementation quality or that Khamenei personally prioritized welfare."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF and the Ministry of Labour discussed a child social-protection system in April 2022, including a welfare database, cash-plus support in selected provinces, and targeting deprived households and vulnerable children.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF and Ministry of Labour discussed enhancing social protection systems for children","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"2022-04-28","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/press-releases/unicef-and-ministry-labour-discussed-enhancing-social-protection-systems-children","claim":"The webinar presented Iran's child social-protection system, a welfare database for deprived households, and a cash-plus model for vulnerable children in selected provinces.","locator":"lines 64-74","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UNICEF press release with specific program mechanics and named government counterparts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous to 2022 and explicitly about children's social protection.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but meaningful; the program ran through Iranian ministries under the regime, though this source does not isolate a Khamenei personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The page describes plans and cooperation, not audited nationwide results."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}
- UNICEF supplied tablets for children with special needs during COVID-era school closures.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF Iran Provides 1,266 Tablets for Students with Special Needs","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/press-releases/unicef-iran-provides-1266-tablets-students-special-needs","claim":"UNICEF provided 1,266 tablets for students with special needs nationwide to support online distance learning during pandemic school closures.","locator":"lines 65-69","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific 2022 press release with a concrete quantity, target group, and delivery mechanism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Close to the target period and tied to the COVID-school-closure legacy still affecting 2022 education access.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; delivered through UNICEF and the Ministry of Education, not a direct Khamenei order.","contrary_evidence":["The action was compensatory and crisis-driven, so it shows mitigation more than structural reform."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF’s school-readiness screening in 2022 expanded to disadvantaged provinces, covered 97,454 children, and gained a dedicated public budget line.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF supports school entrance health screening for 1.5 million children in Iran","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"2023-05-30","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/press-releases/unicef-supports-school-entrance-health-screening-15-million-children-iran","claim":"In 2022 UNICEF covered the screening costs for Afghan refugee children, nomad children, and children in disadvantaged provinces, helping 97,454 children and contributing to a dedicated public budget line and expansion to 750 centres.","locator":"lines 66-70","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The page gives a precise 2022 implementation claim, named provinces, a child count, and institutional follow-on effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The key implementation claim is explicitly about 2022, even though the page was published in 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but useful; it shows state service delivery under the regime, not personally Khamenei’s hand.","contrary_evidence":["The page also shows the service had to be subsidized for disadvantaged groups, implying unequal baseline access."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF’s refugee-education programme in Iran included inclusion for refugees and children with special needs, additional classrooms, WASH upgrades, and resilience planning.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Education for Refugees","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/education-refugees","claim":"UNICEF says it works with Iran to ensure refugees and children with special needs can access education, including through more classrooms, WASH rehabilitation, and inclusive learning tools.","locator":"lines 76-87","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Program page is current rather than time-bounded to 2022, but the operational claims are concrete and consistent with the 2022 program period.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Ongoing program context that plausibly spans 2022, but not a 2022-only snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it reflects government-backed service delivery and access policy, not a distinct Khamenei decision.","contrary_evidence":["Because it is a current program page, it is weaker as exact 2022 evidence than a dated 2022 report."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF’s disability-inclusion page says children with disabilities are a major out-of-school group and describes support for screening, assistive technology, and training.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Access to Inclusive Education for Children with Disabilities","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/access-inclusive-education-children-disabilities","claim":"UNICEF says children with disabilities are a major out-of-school group in Iran and that it is supporting screening, assistive technologies, teacher capacity, and employment transition.","locator":"lines 72-86","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed program page with concrete implementation lines, but it is not dated to 2022 and includes program context beyond the target year.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Useful social-inclusion context; not a clean 2022-only source.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; shows the regime’s service ecosystem and gaps, not a personal Khamenei directive.","contrary_evidence":["The page explicitly says disability-related out-of-school rates remain materially higher than for all primary pupils."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- WHO’s 2022 Iran immunization survey found very high childhood vaccination coverage, while noting non-Iranian children remained below the national average.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Immunization coverage of children aged 24-35 months in the Islamic Republic of Iran: a national cluster coverage survey","publisher":"WHO EMRO / East Mediterr Health J","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://www.emro.who.int/emhj-volume-28-2022/volume-28-issue-2/immunization-coverage-of-children-aged-2435-months-in-the-islamic-republic-of-iran-a-national-cluster-coverage-survey.html","claim":"The survey reported 97.8 percent vaccination coverage for Iranian children and 90.3 percent for non-Iranian children, with 29 provinces at or above 95 percent.","locator":"lines 357-358","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Peer-reviewed WHO regional journal with exact quantitative findings and explicit national-program context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and directly describes Iran's child-immunization system as a service-delivery baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly country-system context; it supports welfare assessment but does not by itself isolate Khamenei's personal contribution.","contrary_evidence":["The non-Iranian coverage gap indicates uneven inclusion even within a strong national program."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- WHO said Iran’s COVID response was hampered by limited genomic sequencing capacity and low vaccination coverage, though WHO and partners later mobilized doses and sequencing support.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Response to COVID-19 in Iran","publisher":"WHO","publication_date":"2022-02-28 best available on page context","url":"https://www.who.int/about/accountability/results/who-results-report-2020-mtr/country-story/2021/iran","claim":"WHO said Iran's pandemic response was hampered by limited genomic sequencing capacity and low vaccination coverage, and that WHO and partners later mobilized vaccine doses and sequencing support.","locator":"lines 217-224","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO country-story page gives a specific assessment of response constraints and remedial support, with explicit dates and quantities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Near-period evidence about a major health crisis still shaping 2022 welfare conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it captures state capacity and crisis response under the regime rather than a direct Khamenei welfare order.","contrary_evidence":["The page also documents external support and eventual scale-up, so it is not a pure failure story."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.5","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF and WFP described refugee school feeding, school construction, fee exemption, and cash incentives for girls in April 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNHCR and WFP help refugee children stay in school and reach for their dreams","publisher":"UNHCR Iran","publication_date":"2022-04-10","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/ir/news/unhcr-and-wfp-help-refugee-children-stay-school-and-reach-their-dreams","claim":"The article says Iran exempts refugees from school fees, WFP provides school snacks and cash incentives for girls, and UNHCR has supported school construction, helping more than 500,000 Afghan children access education.","locator":"lines 127-140","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UNHCR/WFP reporting with concrete service counts and policy details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous to 2022 and tied to refugee welfare and education access.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but meaningful; it reflects state policy and UN-supported delivery under the Iranian regime, not a documented personal Khamenei instruction.","contrary_evidence":["The same article notes that inflation still made school supplies unaffordable for many families, so access remained fragile."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- UNHCR said refugees in Iran received free primary healthcare, including vaccinations and maternal-child services, regardless of documentation status, and that the system had support posts and health workers.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Health","publisher":"UNHCR Iran","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unhcr.org/ir/health","claim":"UNHCR says refugees in Iran can access free primary health care, including vaccinations, essential medicines, and maternal and child health services regardless of documentation status, with support for health posts and health workers.","locator":"lines 125-131","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct operational description from UNHCR with concrete service entitlements and infrastructure support.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Current operational page that reflects the ongoing policy framework relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it shows state-backed access for refugees, but not a personal welfare directive by Khamenei." ,"contrary_evidence":["Because the page is current, it is weaker as a strict 2022 snapshot than a dated 2022 implementation report."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}
- UNFPA said Iran’s maternal mortality rate in 2022 was 23.7 per 100,000 live births and that it supports the health ministry and midwives with evidence-based guidelines.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Maternal Health and Women-Friendly Services","publisher":"UNFPA Iran","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://iran.unfpa.org/en/topics/maternal-health-and-women-friendly-services","claim":"UNFPA estimates Iran's maternal mortality rate at 23.7 per 100,000 lives in 2022 and says it supports the health ministry and midwives with evidence-based guidelines and service strengthening.","locator":"lines 98-100","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"The page is current but contains a direct 2022 estimate and a specific description of ongoing service support.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The mortality estimate is explicitly for 2022; the support description is ongoing context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it reflects health-system quality and public-service support, not a ruler-specific reform order.","contrary_evidence":["The same page notes higher maternal mortality in some provinces and persistent concerns around screening and cesarean rates."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- UNFPA said female economic participation and female-headed-household vulnerability remained weak and that it supported social welfare services for poor women and targeted training.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Women and Girls Empowerment","publisher":"UNFPA Iran","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://iran.unfpa.org/en/topics/women-and-girls-empowerment","claim":"UNFPA says women's economic participation remains far below men's and that it supports social welfare services for poor women, user-friendly information access, and targeted training for female-headed households.","locator":"lines 98-102","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct program description with concrete vulnerability and intervention details, though not a 2022-only dated report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Relevant context for 2022 welfare and gender inclusion, but not a clean dated 2022 snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it describes government-linked social-service gaps and mitigations, not a direct Khamenei decision.","contrary_evidence":["The page itself emphasizes that the underlying labor-market and vulnerability problem remained substantial."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF said the 2021-2022 nutrition survey found widespread zinc and vitamins A/D deficiencies in eight food-insecure provinces, and that it was supporting targeted supplementation and surveillance.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child Nutrition","publisher":"UNICEF Iran","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.unicef.org/iran/en/child-nutrition","claim":"UNICEF says a 2021-2022 survey in eight food-insecure provinces found widespread zinc and vitamins A and D deficiencies among children under five, and that it is supporting surveillance and targeted supplementation.","locator":"lines 74 and 86-90","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"The page contains a specific survey finding and a concrete response plan, but it is not a dated 2022 report page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the target period because the survey spans 2021-2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it shows the welfare environment and program response rather than a ruler-only action.","contrary_evidence":["The page says data and targeting are still inadequate, so service quality and measurement remain weak."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- The World Bank said only a third of pandemic-era job losses had been recovered by April 2022, and that social protection measures lacked targeting and inflation indexation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Iran's Economic Update — April 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-04-14","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/iran/publication/economic-update-april-2022","claim":"The World Bank said only a third of pandemic-period job losses had been recovered and that social-protection measures lacked targeting and inflation indexation, limiting their effect.","locator":"lines 41-45","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous World Bank country update with direct welfare-relevant findings and no need for inference on the economic side.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Country-level welfare context, not a direct Khamenei policy finding, but relevant to life-chances under his rule.","contrary_evidence":["The report acknowledges that social-protection measures did partially mitigate pressure, so it is mixed rather than purely negative."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
- WFP’s February 2022 Iran audit described unconditional food aid, conditional support to refugee women and girls, and emergency food transfers, and concluded the operation was effective/satisfactory.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Internal Audit of WFP Operations in Iran - February 2022","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-03-14","url":"https://www.wfp.org/audit-reports/internal-audit-wfp-operations-iran-february-2022","claim":"WFP's Iran operations included unconditional food assistance, conditional support to refugee women and girls, and emergency food transfers, and the audit concluded the operation was effective/satisfactory.","locator":"lines 265-266","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Internal audit is a strong implementation source, and the relevant service channels are stated explicitly.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous to 2022 and about implementation, not just planning.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; the operation is UN-run with Iranian counterparts, not a documented personal Khamenei initiative.","contrary_evidence":["The audit covers WFP activities, not the entire Iranian welfare state, so it cannot be generalized too far."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 30 distinct documents across UNICEF Iran, UNHCR Iran, UNFPA Iran, WHO, World Bank, WFP, and the World Bank/WHO regional pages.
- I kept dated 2022 items where possible, especially the UNICEF social-protection webinar, the school-tablet release, the WFP audit, the UNHCR/WFP refugee-school article, and the World Bank April 2022 update.
- I kept a few current program pages when they were the clearest place to document the mechanism, but I marked those as context rather than clean 2022-only proof.
- I rejected broader global reports that were not Iran-specific enough, duplicate UNICEF news items that repeated the same service without adding new mechanism detail, and WFP annual-country report PDF links that were blocked by a 403 and could not be opened reliably.
- I also rejected late-2023 and 2024 pages unless they explicitly described 2022 implementation or 2021-2022 surveys, because the target year is 2022.
- I did not re-fetch local structured indicators already provided in the prompt; I used them only as the prior baseline for health, education, HDI, and related outcomes.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct Khamenei-attributed 2022 welfare decisions remain thin. Most evidence is regime-level or ministry/UN partnership level, not a personal welfare agenda order from the Supreme Leader.
- Distribution and dignity evidence is better for refugees, girls, women, and children than for the overall population. More Iran-specific household survey work would help on who benefited versus who was left behind.
- There is still limited hard evidence on whether 2022 social-policy improvements were durable or merely compensatory amid sanctions, inflation, and COVID aftereffects.
- If you want a tighter dossier on 6B.4 and 6B.9, the next useful step would be Persian-language budget/execution records or ministry audits showing whether the 2022 program expansions were actually financed and sustained.
